<!-- ================= PRACTICAL 12 ================= -->
<h1 class="practical-title">Practical 12: Event Management CRUD with Poster Upload and File Validation</h1>

<div class="aim-box">
  <div class="aim-title">AIM:</div>
  Develop event management functionality for admin users. Admin can add, view, update, delete, and upload event posters with file type and file size validation.
</div>

<h2 class="section-heading">1. Implementation Details</h2>
<p>
Event lifecycle management and validated media ingestion were implemented across <code>events.php</code>, <code>event-add.php</code>, <code>event-edit.php</code>, <code>event-delete.php</code>, and <code>event-register.php</code>:
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Poster File Upload Validation:</strong>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>MIME Type Verification:</strong> Evaluated server-side using <code>finfo(FILEINFO_MIME_TYPE)</code> allowing only <code>image/jpeg</code>, <code>image/png</code>, and <code>image/webp</code>.</li>
      <li><strong>Size Enforcement:</strong> Rejects files exceeding 2 MB (2,097,152 bytes).</li>
      <li><strong>Secure Renaming:</strong> Files are renamed using cryptographic hashes (e.g., <code>event_a7b8c9d0e1.jpg</code>) preventing path traversal or filename execution attacks.</li>
      <li><strong>Upload Directory Protection:</strong> An <code>.htaccess</code> file inside <code>uploads/</code> blocks execution of PHP or shell scripts.</li>
    </ul>
  </li>
  <li><strong>Event Catalog & Registration:</strong> Displays events in responsive cards with category badges (Technical, Cultural, Sports, Workshop, Seminar), venue, date, capacity counters, and student one-click registration.</li>
</ul>

<h2 class="section-heading">2. Key Questions / Analysis / Interpretation</h2>
<div class="qa-box">
  <div class="qa-q">Q1. Are add, update, and delete functionalities implemented correctly?</div>
  <div class="qa-a">
    Yes. Administrators can create events with posters, edit metadata, and delete events (which automatically purges associated poster files from disk).
  </div>
</div>

<div class="qa-box">
  <div class="qa-q">Q2. Are poster uploads validated for type and size?</div>
  <div class="qa-a">
    Yes. PHP validates files using genuine file inspection (not just extensions) and strictly rejects files exceeding 2 MB.
  </div>
</div>

<div class="qa-box">
  <div class="qa-q">Q3. Are success and failure messages displayed?</div>
  <div class="qa-a">
    Yes. Flash messages provide feedback on validation errors, successful publications, updates, and cancellations.
  </div>
</div>

<h2 class="section-heading">3. Challenges Faced & Learning Outcomes</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Challenges Faced:</strong> Protecting the server against malicious file uploads masked as images; handling file unlinking on event deletion without leaving orphaned image files.</li>
  <li><strong>Learning Outcomes:</strong> Mastered multipart form handling in PHP, MIME-type inspection with finfo, secure file renaming, and directory access hardening.</li>
</ul>

<div class="repo-footer">
  <strong>Conclusion:</strong> Event Management CRUD with strict file upload validation, secure image storage, and registration capabilities was successfully implemented.<br>
  <strong>Repository Link:</strong> <code>https://github.com/Dev-Jay99/WDF</code>
</div>

<!-- ================= PRACTICAL 13 ================= -->
<h1 class="practical-title">Practical 13: PHP JSON API and AJAX/Fetch-Based CRUD without Page Reload</h1>

<div class="aim-box">
  <div class="aim-title">AIM:</div>
  Create simple PHP JSON API endpoints for events or students and consume them using Fetch API. Perform list, add, update, and delete operations asynchronously without full page reload.
</div>

<h2 class="section-heading">1. Implementation Details</h2>
<p>
Practical 13 decoupled backend data processing from the presentation layer using RESTful JSON APIs and Fetch API:
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>RESTful API Endpoint (<code>api/events.php</code>):</strong>
    <ul>
      <li><code>GET</code>: Fetches all events or a single event by ID with optional search/category filters.</li>
      <li><code>POST</code>: Creates an event, returning HTTP 201 Created and JSON payload.</li>
      <li><code>PUT</code>: Updates an existing event, returning HTTP 200 OK.</li>
      <li><code>DELETE</code>: Deletes an event, returning HTTP 200 OK.</li>
      <li>Outputs strict <code>Content-Type: application/json; charset=UTF-8</code> headers.</li>
    </ul>
  </li>
  <li><strong>Single Page Application Interface (<code>events-ajax.php</code>):</strong>
    <ul>
      <li>Consumes the REST API using <code>fetch()</code> without reloading the browser.</li>
      <li><strong>Debounced Real-Time Search:</strong> A 300ms debounce timer prevents excessive network requests during typing.</li>
      <li><strong>Live DOM Mutation:</strong> Adding or editing updates the table in-place; deleting fades and removes the row with smooth CSS animation.</li>
      <li><strong>Status Notifications:</strong> Toast alerts notify users of API responses and network states.</li>
    </ul>
  </li>
</ul>

<h2 class="section-heading">2. Key Questions / Analysis / Interpretation</h2>
<div class="qa-box">
  <div class="qa-q">Q1. Do PHP endpoints return valid JSON responses?</div>
  <div class="qa-a">
    Yes. All responses are encoded with <code>json_encode()</code> and sent with appropriate HTTP status codes (200, 201, 400, 403, 404, 500).
  </div>
</div>

<div class="qa-box">
  <div class="qa-q">Q2. Are GET and POST requests handled properly?</div>
  <div class="qa-a">
    Yes. The API parses <code>$_SERVER['REQUEST_METHOD']</code>, reads raw JSON streams from <code>php://input</code>, and validates payloads before database execution.
  </div>
</div>

<div class="qa-box">
  <div class="qa-q">Q3. Are loading, success, and error states shown on the frontend?</div>
  <div class="qa-a">
    Yes. Loading skeletons are rendered during network operations, success toasts confirm operations, and error banners report API failure reasons.
  </div>
</div>

<h2 class="section-heading">3. Challenges Faced & Learning Outcomes</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Challenges Faced:</strong> Reading and parsing <code>php://input</code> streams for HTTP PUT and DELETE methods; managing asynchronous DOM updates without race conditions or memory leaks.</li>
  <li><strong>Learning Outcomes:</strong> Mastered RESTful API endpoint design in PHP, HTTP request method routing, Fetch API integration, search debouncing, and single-page asynchronous UI updates.</li>
</ul>

<div class="repo-footer">
  <strong>Conclusion:</strong> Fully asynchronous CRUD operations with RESTful JSON API endpoints, Fetch API, and debounced search were successfully implemented with zero page reloads.<br>
  <strong>Repository Link:</strong> <code>https://github.com/Dev-Jay99/WDF</code>
</div>

<!-- ================= PRACTICAL 14 ================= -->
<h1 class="practical-title">Practical 14: Admin Dashboard with Role-Based Access Control, Dynamic Menu, and Audit Log</h1>

<div class="aim-box">
  <div class="aim-title">AIM:</div>
  Develop an admin dashboard that displays user statistics, student count, event count and latest registrations. Restrict access to admin users only and show dynamic menu options based on role.
</div>

<h2 class="section-heading">1. Implementation Details</h2>
<p>
Administrative intelligence and audit tracking were implemented in <code>admin/dashboard.php</code>, <code>admin/audit-logs.php</code>, and <code>admin/users.php</code>:
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Aggregated KPI Metrics:</strong> Database aggregation queries calculate real-time counts for Active Students, Total Campus Events, Event Registrations, and Authenticated Users.</li>
  <li><strong>Visual Analytics:</strong> Displays proportional progress bars and event breakdown percentages by category (Technical, Cultural, Sports, Workshops, Seminars).</li>
  <li><strong>Dynamic Role-Based Menus:</strong> Global navigation adapts dynamically based on user role—administrators receive links to Admin Dashboard, Student CRUD, Event CRUD, and Audit Logs; students see Profile and Feedback.</li>
  <li><strong>Security Audit Trail (<code>audit_logs</code> table):</strong> All critical actions (logins, logouts, student modifications, event deletions, user toggles) are recorded with timestamps, user IDs, and client IP addresses.</li>
  <li><strong>Intermediate Extension (User Status Toggle):</strong> <code>admin/users.php</code> allows administrators to toggle account status between Active and Inactive with instant effect.</li>
</ul>

<h2 class="section-heading">2. Key Questions / Analysis / Interpretation</h2>
<div class="qa-box">
  <div class="qa-q">Q1. Are dashboard values loaded dynamically from the database?</div>
  <div class="qa-a">
    Yes. KPI summary boxes and registration streams are queried live from MySQL tables on every dashboard load.
  </div>
</div>

<div class="qa-box">
  <div class="qa-q">Q2. Is access restricted to admin users?</div>
  <div class="qa-a">
    Yes. <code>require_admin()</code> guards the dashboard. Unauthorized students or guests are intercepted and served an HTTP 403 Forbidden alert.
  </div>
</div>

<div class="qa-box">
  <div class="qa-q">Q3. Are role-based menus working?</div>
  <div class="qa-a">
    Yes. The navigation bar conditionally renders administrative controls only when <code>$_SESSION['role'] === 'admin'</code>.
  </div>
</div>

<div class="qa-box">
  <div class="qa-q">Q4. Are important admin actions recorded in audit_logs?</div>
  <div class="qa-a">
    Yes. The helper <code>log_audit()</code> writes administrative events into the <code>audit_logs</code> table, visible via the dedicated Audit Log viewer.
  </div>
</div>

<h2 class="section-heading">3. Challenges Faced & Learning Outcomes</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Challenges Faced:</strong> Aggregating counts across multiple relational tables efficiently without slowing down dashboard rendering; preventing self-deactivation of logged-in administrative accounts.</li>
  <li><strong>Learning Outcomes:</strong> Mastered SQL aggregation functions, administrative dashboard design, security audit logging, and role-based interface customization.</li>
</ul>

<div class="repo-footer">
  <strong>Conclusion:</strong> Role-based Admin Dashboard with real-time KPI metrics, dynamic menu switching, user status moderation, and audit trail logging was successfully implemented.<br>
  <strong>Repository Link:</strong> <code>https://github.com/Dev-Jay99/WDF</code>
</div>

<!-- ================= PRACTICAL 15 ================= -->
<h1 class="practical-title">Practical 15: Final Integration, Testing, Documentation, GitHub Submission, and Local Deployment</h1>

<div class="aim-box">
  <div class="aim-title">AIM:</div>
  Integrate all modules into a single deployable StudentHub portal. Verify navigation, responsiveness, authentication, database operations, security checks, AJAX functionality, and documentation. Deploy locally using XAMPP/Apache virtual host and submit GitHub repository.
</div>

<h2 class="section-heading">1. Implementation Details</h2>
<p>
The final capstone practical unified all preceding 14 modules into a deployable application:
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Unified Portal Integration:</strong> Root landing page <code>index.php</code> ties together all modules with dynamic database telemetry, event highlights, external JSON FAQs, and quick links.</li>
  <li><strong>Comprehensive Testing Suite:</strong> Verified zero PHP syntax errors across all 34 codebase scripts via PHP CLI linting. Verified cross-browser responsiveness on mobile, tablet, and desktop viewports.</li>
  <li><strong>Security Verification:</strong> Validated CSRF tokens across all POST actions, 100% prepared statements preventing SQL injection, Bcrypt password hashing, session timeout enforcement, and upload directory script execution blocking.</li>
  <li><strong>Local Deployment:</strong> Configured for deployment on Apache under XAMPP (<code>c:\xampp\htdocs\studenthub\</code>) with database seeds pre-configured in <code>sql/studenthub.sql</code>.</li>
  <li><strong>Documentation & Git Version Control:</strong> Complete <code>README.md</code>, architecture diagrams, installation instructions, default credentials, and clean Git commits covering the full semester curriculum.</li>
</ul>

